# Child-friendly voice

Use a soft, cute, friendly feminine voice that sounds appropriate for a young child. Prefer a naturally youthful voice available through the browser’s speech synthesis voices; select the closest suitable voice and keep the speaking rate gentle and clear. Don’t imitate or clone a real person’s voice. If no suitable voice is available, use the softest, clearest available voice and let a parent choose another voice in settings. Keep the existing speech enable/disable, speed, and volume controls working.

When adding or changing spoken audio:

- Choose the voice in `src/services/speech.ts` (`pickKidsVoice`). Prefer a youthful or soft feminine English voice. Avoid deep adult male voices when a clearer alternative exists.
- Keep the default Parent Settings choice as “Soft voice (recommended)” (`selectedVoice` empty). A parent’s explicit voice choice still wins when that voice is installed. If it is missing, use `resolveVoice` and the on-device fallback. Do not label a voice as a child’s voice unless its name says it is one.
- Pass the existing `speechEnabled`, `speechRate`, and volume settings through. Do not add a new speech on/off, speed, or volume control. Volume applies to narration as well as sound effects.
- Do not raise pitch so far that the voice becomes a cartoon imitation, and do not clone or name a real person.
